<% content_for :title, "Put articles in your app" %>
<% ex = @example %>
<% ex_link = ex ? ex.slug : "your-article" %>
<% ex_title = ex&.live_version&.title || "Help article" %>
<div class="head"><h1>Put articles in your app</h1></div>
<div class="install">
  <p class="lead">Show one article, or the whole help center, inside a page of your app or website with an <code>&lt;iframe&gt;</code>. Only articles readable by <b>Everyone</b> (or <b>Link only</b>, when linked directly) show inside a frame; staff-only articles don't.</p>
  <% unless ex %><p class="hint">Publish an article for Everyone to see real links in these examples.</p><% end %>

  <section>
    <h2>First: allow your site to frame it</h2>
    <p>Browsers only show these pages in an iframe on sites listed in <code>HELPVIBE_FRAME_ANCESTORS</code> on the server. List every site that will embed them, separated by spaces, for example <code>https://app.yourcompany.com https://www.yourcompany.com</code>. The help bubble uses the same list.</p>
    <div class="check <%= @frame_ancestors == "'self'" ? "bad" : "ok" %>">
      <%= icon(@frame_ancestors == "'self'" ? "alert" : "check") %>
      <span>Now set to <code><%= @frame_ancestors %></code>.
        <% if @frame_ancestors == "'self'" %>Iframes on any other site will show a blank or "refused to connect" box until this is changed.<% elsif @frame_ancestors == "*" %>Any site can frame them (fine for development; list your sites in production).<% end %></span>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>One article</h2>
    <p>Just the article: no header, search or menus. Good for onboarding steps or next to a setting it explains. Links inside it open in a new tab.</p>
    <%= render "snippet", code: %(<iframe src="#{embedded_article_url(ex_link)}"\n        title="#{ex_title}" style="width:100%;height:480px;border:0"></iframe>) %>
    <p>To make the frame grow to fit the article instead of scrolling, add this once on the page:</p>
    <%= render "snippet", code: <<~HTML %>
      <script>
        addEventListener("message", function (e) {
          if (e.origin !== #{root_url.chomp("/").to_json} || !e.data || e.data.helpvibe !== "embed-height") return;
          document.querySelectorAll("iframe").forEach(function (f) {
            if (f.contentWindow === e.source) f.style.height = e.data.height + "px";
          });
        });
      </script>
    HTML
    <p class="hint">The address takes the article's link name (<%= ex_link %>) or its code<%= " (#{ex.code})" if ex %>. The code keeps working if the title changes.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>The whole help center</h2>
    <p>The full help center with search and chapters, for a Help page inside your app. "Staff sign in" is hidden inside the frame.</p>
    <p>People can also <b>Contact support</b> from it<% unless Setting.help_center_contact? %> (now turned off in <%= link_to "Articles → Contact support", admin_help_center_path %>)<% end %>: ask a question or file a request (any <%= link_to "customer ticket type", admin_ticket_types_path %>). It lands in the <%= link_to "Inbox", admin_inbox_path %> marked "via help center", and we email them a link back to it. The contact page alone: <code><%= contact_url %></code></p>
    <%= render "snippet", code: %(<iframe src="#{root_url}" title="Help Center"\n        style="width:100%;height:80vh;border:0"></iframe>) %>
    <p>Open it on a chapter, an article or a search instead:</p>
    <table class="table">
      <tr><td>An article<%= " (#{ex.code})" if ex %></td><td><code><%= ex ? article_url(ex) : "#{root_url}a/your-article" %></code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Search</td><td><code><%= search_url(q: "invoice") %></code></td></tr>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Only some collections</h2>
    <p>Add <code>collections=</code> with one or more <%= link_to "collection", admin_collections_path %> link names, separated by commas. The frame then shows only articles in those collections: in lists, chapters, search and related articles, and links to any other article show "not found". It stays that way as people click around inside the frame. Without it, every article shows.</p>
    <%= render "snippet", code: %(<iframe src="#{root_url}?collections=#{@collection_example}" title="Help Center"
        style="width:100%;height:80vh;border:0"></iframe>) %>
    <% if @collections.any? %>
      <p class="hint">Your collections: <% @collections.each_with_index do |c, i| %><%= ", " if i > 0 %><b><%= c.name %></b> <code><%= c.slug %></code><% end %>.</p>
    <% else %>
      <p class="hint">No collections yet. <%= link_to "Create them", admin_collections_path %>, then add articles to them from each article's Details panel.</p>
    <% end %>
    <p class="hint">This picks what a page shows; it doesn't hide anything from someone who edits the address. For articles only some people may read, use <b>Who can see it</b> on the article.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Match your page's colours</h2>
    <p>Framed pages follow the reader's light or dark system setting. To pin one, add <code>?theme=light</code> or <code>?theme=dark</code> to the address; it stays for the rest of the visit inside the frame.</p>
    <%= render "snippet", code: %(<iframe src="#{embedded_article_url(ex_link, theme: "light")}" ...></iframe>) %>
  </section>

  <% if Rails.env.local? %>
    <section>
      <h2>Try it here</h2>
      <p><%= link_to "Open a stand-in app with an article and the help center framed in it", dev_embed_path %>. Development only.</p>
    </section>
  <% end %>

  <section>
    <h2>Other ways</h2>
    <table class="table">
      <tr><td>Help bubble</td><td>Search and page-by-page article suggestions in a corner panel. See <%= link_to "Helpdesk install", admin_install_helpdesk_path %>, then choose articles per page in <%= link_to "Page suggestions", admin_context_rules_path %>.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Open from your button</td><td><code>HelpVibe.open({ article: '<%= ex&.code || "BIL·01" %>' })</code>, once the bubble's script is on the page.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Plain links</td><td><code><%= root_url %></code> and any article or search address above.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>API</td><td>Articles and chapters as JSON, in Helpjuice's format. Create a key in <%= link_to "API and webhooks", admin_api_keys_path %> and keep it on your server.</td></tr>
    </table>
    <%= render "snippet", code: <<~SH %>
      curl -H "Authorization: Bearer YOUR_API_KEY" "#{root_url}api/v3/search?query=invoice"
    SH
  </section>
</div>
